{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Cross-model context (research launched; NO janitor revisions from it - context only, per maintainer). Motivation: the OSS-practice survey found cross-model critique is the one decorrelation mechanism with clean empirical support - Greptile's Model Inversion measured a model reviewing same-model-authored code at 53.7% recall vs 62.0% on another model's code, because 'the types of bugs a model introduces most often are the same types it's more likely to miss during review'; Goel et al. arXiv:2502.04313 report mistakes growing MORE similar as capability rises, and that LLM-judge scores favour models similar to the judge. Our current config is single-family: DEFAULT_MODEL claude-opus-4-8 (frontier), VERIFIER_DEFAULT_MODEL claude-sonnet-4-6 (standard), DEFAULT_BEDROCK_MODEL_ID us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6. Provider is inferred from the model name through pydantic-ai, so a non-Anthropic provider is already architecturally supported - this would be configuration, not new plumbing. Hard constraints on any candidate: Bedrock inference-profile id (plain on-demand ids raise ValidationException), and RELIABLE schema-constrained structured output, since every rebar LLM operation forces JSON-Schema output through pydantic-ai. Key framing recorded for whoever revisits: the goal is decorrelation, not replacement, so a slightly weaker model that fails DIFFERENTLY can be worth more as a reviewer than a stronger model that fails the same way."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786057353737565001,"uuid":"d37ff33d-3697-4432-b1d8-f658cf9adaad"}